+## Getting started
+
+You need Python 3.10 or newer.
+
+```bash
+git clone https://github.com/lawanmuk/Sales-and-Performance-Analysis.git
+cd Sales-and-Performance-Analysis
+python -m venv .venv
+source .venv/bin/activate          # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
+pip install -e ".[dashboard,sql]"
+```
+
+Then try the two main commands:
+
+```bash
+sales-analysis summary     # headline numbers and key insights
+sales-analysis run         # build every chart into visuals/ (add --show to open them)
+```
+
+### Windows on ARM
+
+Some packages, including numpy and pyarrow (used by Streamlit), don't have prebuilt wheels for Windows on ARM yet. If pip tries to build them from source and fails, install the 64-bit Python 3.13 from python.org and create the virtual environment with that. To check which build you're on, run:
+
+```bash
+python -c "import sysconfig; print(sysconfig.get_platform())"
+```
+
+It should print `win-amd64`.
+
+## Dashboard
+
+```bash
+streamlit run app/dashboard.py
+```
+
+Filter by date, region, category and segment. The KPI tiles compare each figure against the previous period of the same length. The tabs cover Overview, Products, Customers, Geography, Shipping and Forecast. You can download the filtered data as a CSV.
+
+## SQL
+
+```bash
+sales-analysis sql --list                  # the eight available queries
+sales-analysis sql top_products            # run one with DuckDB
+sales-analysis sql rfm_segments --show-sql # print the SQL as well
+```
+
+The queries live in `src/sales_analysis/queries/`. They use window functions, CTEs and NTILE scoring, and they return the same numbers as the pandas code. Each one reads the CSV through a `sales` view, so you can also open the files in the DuckDB CLI or a tool like DBeaver.
+
+## Forecast
+
+```bash
+sales-analysis forecast               # six months ahead
+sales-analysis forecast --months 12   # or any other horizon
+```
+
+The two models are trained on 2015 to 2017 and scored on 2018. Holt-Winters (a damped trend with yearly seasonality) beat seasonal naive with growth, with a MAPE of 18.1% against 28.7%. That's the model used for the forecast. The forecast for 2019:
+
+| Month    | Forecast | Lower    | Upper    |
+| -------- | -------- | -------- | -------- |
+| Jan 2019 | $49,033  | $31,677  | $66,389  |
+| Feb 2019 | $41,195  | $23,839  | $58,551  |
+| Mar 2019 | $72,166  | $54,810  | $89,522  |
+| Apr 2019 | $60,576  | $43,220  | $77,932  |
+| May 2019 | $67,529  | $50,172  | $84,885  |
+| Jun 2019 | $62,588  | $45,232  | $79,944  |
+
+The Lower and Upper columns form an 80% band, about $17,400 on either side of each forecast. It's based on the typical miss on the 2018 holdout. Four years of monthly data is a small sample for seasonality, so treat the band as a rough guide.
+
+![Sales forecast](visuals/sales_forecast.png)
+
+## Charts
+
+Running `sales-analysis run` writes these to `visuals/`:
+
+- `monthly_sales.png`, `yearly_growth.png`, `seasonality_heatmap.png`
+- `sales_by_category.png`, `sales_by_region.png`, `sales_by_segment.png`, `subcategory_sales.png`
+- `top_products.png`, `pareto_curve.png`, `rfm_segments.png`
+- `shipping_times.png`, `sales_forecast.png`
+- `sales_by_state_map.html` and `top_products_interactive.html`, which open in any browser
+
+## Data
+
+The sales file is `data/train.csv`. The cleaning step in `src/sales_analysis/data.py`:
+
+- reads order dates as day, month, year
+- zero pads postal codes to five digits
+- removes duplicate order lines (rows identical apart from the Row ID)
+- adds Year, Month, Quarter and Ship Days columns
+
+If your file lives somewhere else, pass `--data path/to/file.csv` to any command.
+
+## Project layout
+
+```
+app/dashboard.py          Streamlit dashboard
+data/                     the sales CSV
+src/sales_analysis/
+  cli.py                  command line entry point
+  data.py                 loading and cleaning
+  metrics.py              aggregations (no plotting)
+  plots.py                chart functions
+  filters.py              dashboard filters and previous period
+  forecast.py             models, backtest and forecast
+  sql.py                  DuckDB runner
+  queries/                SQL files
+tests/                    pytest suite and a small hand checked dataset
+visuals/                  generated charts
+```
+
+## Tests and quality
+
+```bash
+pytest                                   # 55 tests, 93% coverage
+ruff check . && ruff format --check .    # lint and format check
+pre-commit install                       # run the checks before every commit
+```
+
+GitHub Actions runs the lint check, the test suite on Ubuntu and Windows with Python 3.11 and 3.13, and then rebuilds the charts.
+
